      "math_academy_id": "q-21026",
      "sequence_position": 34,
      "problem": "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+8$ is strictly increasing.",
      "worked_solution": "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-3\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-6x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n6x(x-1)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=6x(x-1)$ has roots at $x=0$ and $x=1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14110945/assets/ec7ea6baa82d60a6cf472c904460190042d889d86cb092b19d9809111bd93b36.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14110945/repairs/completeness-7d497d86b4f7436d/source/8ac550707a6327087f569546e18325066e3e031cb6202bc06c702df00b4fcfc6.html",
